INT. STAGE STATION KITCHEN, DRY CREEK - LATE MORNING

A one-room adobe with a cuckoo clock that has run seven minutes slow since the Grant administration. Flies drift in the heat. A cast-iron stove ticks as it cools.

ODELL PRUITT, fifties, stationmaster, wipes a tin cup with a rag that is dirtier than the cup. At the table MAUDE TEAGUE, thirties, a seed-and-feed saleswoman in a dust-grey travelling dress, peels a boiled egg. ZEBEDIAH HOLLOWAY, called ZEB, eighty, hat on indoors, whittles a stick down to nothing.

ODELL
Coach is late.

MAUDE
Coach is always late.

ZEB
Wheel, probably.

MAUDE
Or the Piute crossing.

ODELL
It's never the Piute crossing.

ZEB
Might be the Piute crossing.

A horn sounds, faint and flat, out on the road.

BARNABY (O.S.)
(far off)
Hey the station! Hey, Odell!

ODELL
There. Wheel.

BARNABY (O.S.)
(nearer)
Axle! Lost the axle at Mesquite Wash!

MAUDE
I told you it was a wheel.

ZEB
Axle's near enough.

Odell sets down the cup and takes the egg shell Maude offers without thinking. She sets the egg on the table. Zeb finishes whittling and examines the empty air where the stick was.

ODELL
(to himself)
Another night, then.

MAUDE
Is there stew?

BARNABY (O.S.)
Is there stew?

ODELL
There's stew.

ZEB
There's always stew.

The cuckoo clock coughs, produces one grey wooden bird, and retires without a sound.
